WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.202 --> 00:00:04.118
[jets roaring]
[fire explodes]

00:00:04.118 --> 00:00:05.970
Vincent Aiello: Oh,
no, James Bond is dead.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:09.300
Oh, no, wait! He flies through
the fireball unscathed!

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.900
Hello, my name is Vincent Aiello.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.510
I am a retired United
States Navy fighter pilot.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:15.242
Spent about 25 years in service,

00:00:15.242 --> 00:00:17.990
flying mainly the F/A-18
Hornet and Super Hornet.

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:21.590
Also had a chance to fly the
F-16 Fighting Falcon, or Viper.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:24.120
Had a tour as a TOPGUN instructor.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.800
And we are going to take a look

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.980
at flying scenes from 10 popular movies.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.386
Steve: Jimmy, he's closing on you!

00:00:34.386 --> 00:00:37.430
[machine beeping]

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:38.338
Put your mask back on!

00:00:38.338 --> 00:00:39.321
Vincent: In our first scene here,

00:00:39.321 --> 00:00:41.796
Harry Connick Jr. turned fighter pilot

00:00:41.796 --> 00:00:43.750
is struggling with his mask.

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:46.550
The masks that we wear
are very constricting.

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:48.620
Now, granted, they're
providing 100% oxygen

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:50.750
at positive pressure, which is great.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.940
But they're still on your face,

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.411
and they're constricting,

00:00:54.411 --> 00:00:56.490
and you can get a little claustrophobic.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.960
Of course, once you've flown
awhile, you get used to them,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.675
but if you begin hyperventilating,

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:01.975
you might feel like you just need

00:01:01.975 --> 00:01:03.383
to release some of that pressure,

00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:05.113
and I believe that's
what's happening here.

00:01:05.113 --> 00:01:07.780
[machine beeps]

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.500
Yeah, they might have taken some liberties

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.120
with the maneuverability
of the F-18 down low

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.820
and how difficult that can be

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:18.700
when you don't know exactly
what the canyon does,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.560
whether it just turns and stops

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.859
or turns 40 degrees, 90 degrees.

00:01:23.859 --> 00:01:25.700
[jet engine roaring]
[machine beeping]

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.400
This is not a display
that we have in the F-18.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.060
The reserve fuel, which we don't have.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.984
When you get to zero, you're done.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.560
We don't have drogue chutes on the F-18

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.210
to slow it down like a B-52 or an F-5,

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:46.010
but it's compelling to
jettison the drogue chute

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:48.400
into the face of the attacking alien.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:50.820
When you're almost out of fuel
in an aircraft, you eject,

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.020
and then ideally you're
gonna go high enough

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.693
to not hit the wall yourself.

00:01:56.712 --> 00:01:57.927
[crashes]

00:01:57.927 --> 00:01:59.600
[makes crash noise]
[laughs]

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.140
He just crumbles into
this big puddle of man.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:04.790
You survive the ejection

00:02:04.790 --> 00:02:07.210
only to break your back on landing.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.790
In flight school, every aviator is taught

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:12.230
a PLF, a parachute landing fall.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.890
You wanna land on your feet
with a little bit of a side

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:15.910
and then go to your ankles,

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.715
your calves, your hip,

00:02:17.715 --> 00:02:20.117
your side, and your shoulders,

00:02:20.117 --> 00:02:22.677
and you wanna kind of
roll it out, almost like

00:02:22.677 --> 00:02:25.560
maybe what a karate maneuver
would be if you were thrown.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.610
My guess is they took an actual parachute

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.930
and probably a 200-pound dummy
and had him falling here,

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.840
which is why he's just
kinda hanging there limp

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:34.090
like a scarecrow.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:37.575
Fun movie, but I'm gonna
give it a three out of 10.

00:02:37.575 --> 00:02:40.129
Merlin: Ice is right below us.
He's got a MiG on his tail.

00:02:40.129 --> 00:02:42.179
[jet engine roaring]

00:02:42.179 --> 00:02:43.830
He's firing! Break left, break left!

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.770
[machine gun firing]

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:48.480
Vincent: Now, the names that
characters in aviation movies

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.180
call each other, actually, those are real.

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:51.578
Those are called call signs.

00:02:51.578 --> 00:02:53.770
They're basically a
fancy name for nicknames.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:56.765
Besides being fun and
funny, these call signs,

00:02:56.765 --> 00:02:59.170
or nicknames, they're
actually really useful.

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:04.039
They depersonalize the
situation, and they remove rank.

00:03:04.039 --> 00:03:04.872
Slider: You got a MiG on your left.

00:03:04.872 --> 00:03:06.130
On your left! 3 o'clock.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.030
Vincent: Talking about every
little thing you're doing

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:09.800
is just gonna clobber the radios.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.090
It's gonna make it very difficult

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:13.480
for someone to say the
things that are important

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:14.700
that need to be said,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.270
i.e., "on defensive," or, "fox three,"

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.300
if you're taking an active missile shot,

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.874
"fox two," for an infrared shot.

00:03:22.874 --> 00:03:24.945
[jet engine roaring]

00:03:24.945 --> 00:03:26.570
Controller: Maverick's reengaging, sir.

00:03:28.070 --> 00:03:29.710
Vincent: Now, you see
Maverick decide to go ahead

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.640
and get back in the
fight, and the controller

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:33.620
on the ship instantly recognizes that,

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:36.820
just because he happens to
turn left a few degrees.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:40.185
The radar, the air-search radar
on the Nimitz-class carrier

00:03:40.185 --> 00:03:43.770
is the AN/SPS-48. It's
an air-search radar,

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:45.490
has about a 200-mile range,

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:47.420
and at its highest rotation rate

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:49.586
has about 15 revolutions per minute,

00:03:49.586 --> 00:03:51.453
or one every four seconds.

00:03:51.453 --> 00:03:53.850
Well, at that range,
with that update rate,

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.890
there's no possible way this controller

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.360
understands that Maverick is
suddenly back in the fight

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:00.162
just because he happens to roll left

00:04:00.162 --> 00:04:01.862
a few degrees and maneuver back.

00:04:01.862 --> 00:04:02.919
Merlin: Break left, break left!

00:04:02.919 --> 00:04:06.009
[machine gun firing]

00:04:08.450 --> 00:04:12.760
Vincent: Now, the "MiG-28" in
the scene is played by an F-5.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:14.740
A couple F-5s. One is a two-seater.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:17.920
Now, the F-5 Freedom Fighter,
better known as the Tiger II,

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.610
in real life has a twin barrel cannon

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:21.750
mounted on the front of the nose,

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.210
not a Gatling gun style as depicted,

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:25.960
and it might make a little bit more

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:28.353
of that standard machine-gun-style sound.

00:04:28.353 --> 00:04:31.673
But in reality, for what they
depicted, that Gatling gun

00:04:31.673 --> 00:04:33.840
is gonna come out with a
much higher rate of fire.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.390
I've never seen an aircraft,
even Russian aircraft,

00:04:41.390 --> 00:04:44.170
that are controlled with the left hand.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:46.119
Iceman: I got him, Mav.
One MiG passing between us.

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:47.842
[jet engine roaring]

00:04:47.842 --> 00:04:49.890
Vincent: There's a
couple editing mistakes.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.487
So, example, you hear Iceman say,

00:04:51.487 --> 00:04:53.007
"He's passing between us."

00:04:53.007 --> 00:04:55.340
And it's a MiG-28 that passes between us,

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:58.180
and then you see from
Maverick's point of view

00:04:58.180 --> 00:04:59.870
two aircraft with one go down the middle.

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:03.240
Well, the other aircraft
is another MiG-28 or F-5,

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.375
and so that didn't really make sense.

00:05:05.375 --> 00:05:06.208
[machine beeping]

00:05:06.208 --> 00:05:07.041
Slider: You got a MiG on your left.

00:05:07.041 --> 00:05:08.257
On your left! 3 o'clock.

00:05:08.257 --> 00:05:10.610
Vincent: Slider in the back seat, saying,

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.660
"Check the guys at the left, 3 o'clock."

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.070
Well, he's looking out the right side,

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:15.920
and in fact, "right 3 o'clock"

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:17.920
would have been the
right thing to say there.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.927
Iceman: I'm on his tail. I'm going for it.

00:05:19.927 --> 00:05:21.950
Vincent: Ice is shouting,
"I'm on his tail."

00:05:21.950 --> 00:05:25.600
And then the very next clip
is the F-5 chasing the F-14,

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.010
where you can clearly see

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.920
it's the F-5 that's behind
the F-14 and not vice versa.

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.730
So, overall, "Top Gun" of
course is a fan favorite.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.720
I've enjoyed it since I was a young man,

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.861
and I'll have to give this scene,

00:05:36.861 --> 00:05:39.136
let's call it a seven out of 10.

00:05:39.136 --> 00:05:42.219
[machine gun firing]

00:05:46.185 --> 00:05:48.951
I love that they brought in the F-35B.

00:05:48.951 --> 00:05:50.920
Relatively new when they filmed this.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.378
But my question is,

00:05:52.378 --> 00:05:56.086
if you had a high-power rifle

00:05:56.086 --> 00:05:58.860
and almost unlimited rounds,

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:00.810
and you could employ against someone

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:03.143
you're trying to take care of

00:06:03.143 --> 00:06:06.460
from even 50 feet, 100 feet,

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:09.700
why would you go up and get
in a phone booth with them,

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.866
where now the gun is unwieldy,

00:06:11.866 --> 00:06:13.170
and he can turn around you

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.480
and stick you in the back with a knife?

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:16.900
You've got the advantage.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:19.220
He could have gone
about a half a mile away

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:23.400
and seen the entire scene
in his heads-up display.

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.780
You know, we see examples of people

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.470
trying to hold onto aircraft.
I've never tried this.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.330
Now, I do know from walking around

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:31.930
on top of the F/A-18 Hornet on occasion

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:34.250
that the surfaces, well, they're smooth,

00:06:34.250 --> 00:06:36.650
and there are various nooks
and crannies and openings

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.170
that someone could get a handhold,

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.170
a lot like a rock climber on a wall,

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:43.850
but add to that the
acceleration of moving aircraft

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:46.210
and the momentum and
the wind and all that,

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.730
this would be a pretty difficult task.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:51.870
I'm gonna give you a one
out of 10 for this scene.

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:54.945
Doug Masters: And you can
deal with me, Doug Masters.

00:06:54.945 --> 00:06:57.660
[jet engine roaring]

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:58.993
Ted Masters: Watch it, son!

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.130
Doug: Hey, nice shot, colonel.

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.490
Anything else you wanna send over?

00:07:03.490 --> 00:07:04.940
Ted: Way to fly, Doug.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:07.320
Vincent: That Hollywood
thinks a simple aileron roll

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:09.610
is going to defend your aircraft

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:11.000
from almost anything that's happening,

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.267
somebody pursuing you, a shot at you —

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:16.360
"If you just do a quick
aileron roll, you're fine."

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.760
Yeah, not so much.

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.156
Akir Nakesh: Time to die, Iron Eagle.

00:07:21.156 --> 00:07:22.250
[gun fires]

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:23.820
Vincent: I don't understand
why they can't get

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:24.930
the gun sounds correct.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.660
I mean, I guess we're
all conditioned to hear

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:29.070
the individual bullets sounding,

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:32.362
but, in fact, with a high rate of fire,

00:07:32.362 --> 00:07:35.329
like the M-61 cannon and the F-14,

00:07:35.329 --> 00:07:37.530
it fires 6,000 rounds a minute.

00:07:37.530 --> 00:07:39.050
That's 100 rounds a second.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:40.450
You don't hear individual bullets;

00:07:40.450 --> 00:07:42.506
you just hear that "zzz" sound.

00:07:42.506 --> 00:07:43.860
Ted: You can do it.

00:07:45.210 --> 00:07:48.340
Vincent: When an aircraft is
directly at your 6 o'clock,

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:52.950
there's almost nothing harder
for the defender in this case.

00:07:52.950 --> 00:07:55.530
Because no matter what
you do theoretically,

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:56.910
the other aircraft can follow you.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.197
I mean, as long as the performance is not

00:07:59.197 --> 00:08:01.955
grossly worse in the attacking aircraft.

00:08:01.955 --> 00:08:03.988
And yet, we have this scene where dad,

00:08:03.988 --> 00:08:05.560
being the good dad that he is,

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:06.697
just some happy talk,

00:08:06.697 --> 00:08:08.030
"Come on, Doug, you can do it.

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:11.242
Let's take care of this guy.
Let's take this guy out."

00:08:11.242 --> 00:08:13.938
And magically, the next moment,

00:08:13.938 --> 00:08:18.020
we've got Doug in the
F-16 behind the bad guy.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:21.030
A dogfight, you're limited by physics.

00:08:21.030 --> 00:08:22.573
I mean, that's a dumb
thing to say, but you are.

00:08:22.573 --> 00:08:26.250
I mean, you can't just
instantaneously swap positions

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.770
traveling 200 to 400 miles per hour.

00:08:28.770 --> 00:08:32.010
You can't just suddenly
trade spots, hit the brakes,

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:33.369
and he'll fly right by.

00:08:34.243 --> 00:08:37.880
Heads-up display actually
is real F-16 HUD Symbology.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:38.900
We don't have to go through all of it,

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.255
but you can see air speed and altitude,

00:08:41.255 --> 00:08:43.421
you can see heading, you can see G.

00:08:43.421 --> 00:08:45.037
The one thing that they mix up, though,

00:08:45.037 --> 00:08:47.060
is towards the bottom off to the left,

00:08:47.060 --> 00:08:49.383
you see the letters SIM.

00:08:49.383 --> 00:08:52.410
That's SIM. That means you're
in a simulated war mode.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:54.732
That's what we used to do in training.

00:08:54.732 --> 00:08:56.323
I'm gonna give it a three out of 10 though

00:08:56.323 --> 00:08:58.123
for this particular scene.

00:08:58.123 --> 00:09:01.201
[jet engine roaring]

00:09:03.138 --> 00:09:04.990
James Marshall: These MiGs,
how far away are they?

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:08.898
Vincent: The F-15s love to fly

00:09:08.898 --> 00:09:10.570
in what's called an Eagle Wall.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:12.850
They might have as many
as 12 eagles in this wall,

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:14.300
but what they won't be

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:16.800
is right next to each
other, as you see depicted.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:19.190
They might be a mile,
up to two miles apart

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:21.780
off each other's
altitude, because that way

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:24.400
they can't be so easily targeted.

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:26.300
Then you see the afterburners stage in,

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:27.960
and of course Hollywood loves to show

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.533
any fighter jet ever in afterburner.

00:09:30.533 --> 00:09:32.590
You gotta have some orange glow behind it.

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:34.073
But the acceleration here,

00:09:34.073 --> 00:09:36.256
probably a little bit artificial.

00:09:36.256 --> 00:09:38.960
Yes, afterburner does
provide some extra thrust,

00:09:38.960 --> 00:09:40.870
and it's wonderful, it's a lot of fun,

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:43.870
but it's not gonna be like a drag car

00:09:43.870 --> 00:09:46.060
coming off the green light there.

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:48.300
When we go into combat,
one of the things we do is

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:49.758
we have what is called FENCE check,

00:09:49.758 --> 00:09:51.360
and you quote, "FENCE-IN."

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.010
And one of the things you do is

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:55.080
you turn off your anti-collision lights,

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:57.460
and at night you turn
off your position lights,

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:00.058
only leaving those
night-vision-goggle-type

00:10:00.058 --> 00:10:01.533
strip lights of green.

00:10:01.533 --> 00:10:05.160
Both the F-15s in this
case and the MiG-29s

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.722
are shown with their navigation lights.

00:10:07.722 --> 00:10:10.223
Caldwell: Coming at us.
Countermeasures are activated.

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:16.090
Vincent: Next you see the VC-25,

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.600
which is the name of the,
what we would all call a 747.

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.007
Air Force One, don't forget,

00:10:21.007 --> 00:10:22.831
is just the title of that aircraft

00:10:22.831 --> 00:10:24.810
when the president is on board.

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:26.950
The defensive systems on a VC-25

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:29.270
are not well publicized.
Of course, why would they?

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:30.870
They don't want opponents to know

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:32.790
what the president's
protective measures are.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:34.420
I assume there are some sort of

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:36.543
expendables like chaff and flares.

00:10:38.741 --> 00:10:41.074
[gun fires]

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:44.440
Why not just fire a bunch more missiles,

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.090
or why not just sit back
there and fire the gun?

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:49.990
Instead, the next thing we
see is this slashing attack

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:53.100
over the right wing, where, oh, lucky him,

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.050
it hit the No. 4 engine.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:56.717
Here's the crazy part: OK, you've got

00:10:56.717 --> 00:10:58.980
this lieutenant-colonel
sitting right next to you.

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:01.210
If that's a first officer at my airline,

00:11:01.210 --> 00:11:02.280
I'm a first officer,

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:04.630
I'm fully qualified to fly that airplane.

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:07.730
I'm not gonna sit there and
wonder where the fire light is

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:09.620
or have somebody on the ground tell me

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:11.840
we need to trim out the aircraft

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:14.180
because — with the rudder trim here.

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:15.517
This guy should be able to say,

00:11:15.517 --> 00:11:17.525
"Hey, president, I got this, sir."

00:11:19.150 --> 00:11:21.980
The F-15s firing what I
presume would be AMRAAMs.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:24.033
That's the AIM-120 air-to-air missile.

00:11:25.016 --> 00:11:26.099
Pilot: Fox three.

00:11:27.721 --> 00:11:28.746
Vincent: They call fox three,

00:11:28.746 --> 00:11:30.150
which is the right terminology.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:31.760
The rules of engagement, clearly,

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.750
we are in a self-defense situation.

00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:37.640
Lots of exaggerations and liberties taken,

00:11:37.640 --> 00:11:40.670
shall we say, from the point
of view of military aviation.

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:42.728
We're gonna give this
one a four out of 10.

00:11:48.262 --> 00:11:50.929
[explosion]

00:11:53.933 --> 00:11:56.440
I've not tried this, but I'm pretty sure

00:11:56.440 --> 00:11:57.993
air-breathing jet aircraft

00:11:57.993 --> 00:12:00.670
don't fly too well
through massive fireballs.

00:12:00.670 --> 00:12:02.750
Plus, we're always dripping hydraulics

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:05.280
or fluids of some sort
that are usually flammable.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:07.064
So not something you wanna do.

00:12:09.090 --> 00:12:11.250
Of course, note to self,
if you ever knock out

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.170
the back seater, make
sure you spend the time

00:12:13.170 --> 00:12:14.570
to get him out of your back seat.

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:16.150
[guns firing]

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:18.310
He's kinda shaking from the recoil,

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:20.702
and I just,

00:12:20.702 --> 00:12:23.200
I've never flown an L-39 with gun pods.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:25.720
I can't imagine though that you're gonna

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:28.416
feel the recoil like a
machine gunner would.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:31.620
And he's flying with his knees.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:34.620
Now, I actually did this
quite often in the F-18.

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:36.918
The F-18 has a center stick,

00:12:36.918 --> 00:12:39.560
and it's conveniently located
right between your knees.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:42.240
The F-16, on the other
hand, has a side stick.

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:44.960
Now, in the F-18, if I were
busy with both of my hands —

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:47.950
I never had to defend from
someone trying to strangle me.

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:49.830
But if there were times when I needed

00:12:49.830 --> 00:12:52.920
to adjust my helmet or
maybe I was doing something

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:54.760
with the throttle and
I was writing something

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:57.580
on my knee board, then
I could, for a moment,

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.950
fly the aircraft with my knees.

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:02.072
Of course, in Hollywood, anytime you have

00:13:02.072 --> 00:13:04.488
the main character in an aircraft,

00:13:04.488 --> 00:13:06.063
you'll always be able to see his face.

00:13:06.063 --> 00:13:08.960
No masks. Maybe a clear visor,

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:10.460
but you'll never have that dark visor

00:13:10.460 --> 00:13:12.290
like you're just some random bad guy.

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:13.598
This flying scene,

00:13:13.598 --> 00:13:15.131
I'm gonna give it a two out of 10.

00:13:15.131 --> 00:13:16.253
Sorry, James Bond.

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.100
They actually went out, like they did

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:25.350
on "Top Gun" and other movies,

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:28.880
and filmed real flying scenes
on a real aircraft carrier,

00:13:28.880 --> 00:13:30.560
in this case the USS Nimitz.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.070
You have real F-14s,
you have real ordinance,

00:13:33.070 --> 00:13:34.196
you have real deck personnel.

00:13:34.196 --> 00:13:35.760
They're not gonna bother with actors.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.500
The AIM-54 Phoenix that they depict there,

00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:42.550
now, the yellow band is a way
to know for a live warhead,

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:44.660
and that's why it's on the forward part.

00:13:44.660 --> 00:13:48.550
And a brown band tells you
it's a live rocket motor.

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:51.730
So when you see yellow and
brown on that white missile,

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:53.200
which, the white doesn't really matter,

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:56.200
you know that's a real war shot AIM-54.

00:13:56.200 --> 00:13:58.570
Now, if it was white
but with blue stripes,

00:13:58.570 --> 00:13:59.868
blue means inert.

00:13:59.868 --> 00:14:02.100
Or if the whole weapon
happened to be blue,

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.244
then you would know that that
was an inert weapon as well.

00:14:04.244 --> 00:14:07.050
[jet engine roaring]

00:14:07.050 --> 00:14:10.830
Here we have the F-14 pilot getting

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:13.750
what might only be considered a real kill.

00:14:13.750 --> 00:14:15.620
Frankly, a zero is probably traveling

00:14:15.620 --> 00:14:18.010
about 120 miles an hour, maybe.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.750
F-14 looks like it's doing about 300.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:23.090
That's gonna be a tough shot, frankly,

00:14:23.090 --> 00:14:26.390
for the F-14 pilot, but
given the correct position

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:27.765
and getting slow enough,

00:14:27.765 --> 00:14:30.664
then that could be very easily done.

00:14:32.269 --> 00:14:35.352
[jet engine roaring]

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:40.180
Here you have a red shirt

00:14:40.180 --> 00:14:41.550
that's dealing with the arresting wire.

00:14:41.550 --> 00:14:42.700
Usually that's a green shirt.

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:45.070
I'm not sure why they
used a red shirt there.

00:14:45.070 --> 00:14:46.730
Maybe it was someone under training.

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:48.200
But normally the different shirts

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:50.278
on the flight deck are important

00:14:50.278 --> 00:14:52.070
because that way when you can't hear,

00:14:52.070 --> 00:14:54.688
you can still tell what someone's job is.

00:14:54.688 --> 00:14:58.160
So, red is ordinance. Green
is catapult and arresting gear

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:00.090
as well as squadron maintenance.

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:02.870
Blue are the chock and chain handlers.

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:06.780
White is safety and LSOs.
Brown is plane captains.

00:15:06.780 --> 00:15:08.240
Yellow are aircraft directors.

00:15:08.240 --> 00:15:09.670
You've got all these different colors.

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.995
I'm gonna give this
scene an eight out of 10.

00:15:11.995 --> 00:15:13.535
Just docking a couple of points.

00:15:13.535 --> 00:15:16.618
[jet engine roaring]

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:22.050
He's gotta fly away pretty quickly.

00:15:22.050 --> 00:15:24.540
I mean, the BD-5J
Microjet's got a top speed

00:15:24.540 --> 00:15:26.423
of about 320 miles per hour.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:30.980
This whole notion of missiles

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:32.910
that can fly next to you

00:15:32.910 --> 00:15:37.020
and continue to pursue
you until they kill you,

00:15:37.020 --> 00:15:39.070
now, in real life, a
surface-to-air missile

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:40.340
such as this is gonna go up to

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:43.530
maybe close to 2,000 miles per hour.

00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:45.330
It's going to be able to go after it,

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:46.510
and it's ether gonna hit it

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:48.540
or get close enough to proximity detonate

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:50.590
and take it out, but
what it is not gonna do

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:51.767
is come over here and say,

00:15:51.767 --> 00:15:53.400
"Oh, yeah? Well, I'm gonna get you,"

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:54.740
and just keep following you around,

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.250
and if it does miss get mad and come back.

00:15:57.250 --> 00:15:59.860
The whole idea that we have these angry

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:02.920
missiles that can follow us,
in this movie and others,

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:04.240
it just always struck me as funny

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:06.160
because in order to do that

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.167
it would have to fly at
roughly the same speed.

00:16:08.167 --> 00:16:09.425
[jet engine roaring]

00:16:09.425 --> 00:16:13.690
Just because an aircraft,
and any aircraft, is diving,

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:15.910
doesn't mean it's going to sound

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:19.142
like a World War II era Stuka dive bomber.

00:16:19.142 --> 00:16:19.975
Guess what?

00:16:19.975 --> 00:16:23.160
They put particular
sirens on those aircraft

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:25.530
that were wind driven,
and at a certain speed

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.210
they did wind up like that,

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:31.530
and, yes, we all associate
that with death from the skies.

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:33.560
Now, the fuel light here,
actually, I love this,

00:16:33.560 --> 00:16:37.370
because the Microjet only
carries 30 gallons of fuel.

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:38.440
Still love the scene,

00:16:38.440 --> 00:16:40.430
so I'm going to give this opening scene

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:42.887
of "Octopussy" a four out of 10.

00:16:42.887 --> 00:16:44.070
[guns firing]

00:16:44.070 --> 00:16:45.560
Hal Moore: Broken arrow!

00:16:46.406 --> 00:16:49.730
I'll say it again, broken arrow!

00:16:49.730 --> 00:16:51.713
Vincent: "Broken arrow" is a term used for

00:16:51.713 --> 00:16:54.429
a nuclear weapon that
has been compromised.

00:16:54.429 --> 00:16:55.910
Maybe it's either been lost

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:58.040
or dropped inadvertently
or something else.

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:00.710
Apparently it does mean we
are either being overrun

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:02.373
or in threat of being overrun,

00:17:02.373 --> 00:17:04.730
but the idea is that
it calls up everybody.

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:06.770
It's like calling 911.

00:17:06.770 --> 00:17:10.313
As he's talking, it depicts
them coming in and attacking.

00:17:10.313 --> 00:17:11.770
There is some coordination that exists,

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:14.730
because the whole idea
of close air support

00:17:14.730 --> 00:17:16.940
is that you are in close coordination

00:17:16.940 --> 00:17:18.040
with the folks on the ground.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:20.014
You are dropping right next to them,

00:17:20.014 --> 00:17:21.840
and in fact, as we see in this scene,

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:24.006
sometimes it can happen
that it's too close.

00:17:24.006 --> 00:17:25.320
[jet engine roaring]

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:26.850
What you're seeing here is napalm.

00:17:26.850 --> 00:17:29.260
It's nondiscriminating,
as most weapons are.

00:17:29.260 --> 00:17:31.190
But if dropped in the wrong place,

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:32.920
it's gonna burn up anything.

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:34.910
And the one thing they
didn't quite get right here

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:37.970
is a Mark 77, or a napalm canister,

00:17:37.970 --> 00:17:41.220
is deliberately not equipped with fins,

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:43.890
because what fins do is
put a spin on a weapon

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:44.831
to make it more accurate,

00:17:44.831 --> 00:17:46.322
for the same reason a quarterback

00:17:46.322 --> 00:17:48.470
puts a spin on a football
when he throws it.

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:49.620
I feel like they're showing

00:17:49.620 --> 00:17:52.350
the releasing aircraft
just a little too low.

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:55.060
It shows fighters going both directions.

00:17:55.060 --> 00:17:57.040
That might be a little
bit of an exaggeration.

00:17:57.040 --> 00:17:58.480
Generally you don't want that

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:01.800
because they could employ
against each other by accident.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:03.630
I'm gonna give it a nine out of 10.

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:04.930
Just loses a few points

00:18:04.930 --> 00:18:06.575
for the reasons we talked about.

00:18:06.575 --> 00:18:08.510
Pilot: Lime zero one, we
got a tally on three trucks.

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:10.410
Eastbound on the bridge.

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:13.050
Harry: Roger, Lime Zero
One. Prepare to engage.

00:18:13.050 --> 00:18:15.700
Pilot: Ringo is padlocked.
In hot with guns.

00:18:16.550 --> 00:18:18.640
Vincent: What bothers me
though about this scene

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:22.770
is this expectation almost in Hollywood

00:18:22.770 --> 00:18:24.280
that they can feed the audience

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:27.680
that somewhere in America,
even off the coast of Key West,

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:29.200
as depicted in this scene,

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:32.130
are fully armed aircraft
just flying around,

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:33.280
doing maneuvers.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:35.440
In reality, particularly since 9/11,

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:37.870
we do have aircraft
that are armed on alert

00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:40.460
at various bases, usually on the coasts,

00:18:40.460 --> 00:18:42.480
and that is for Noble Eagle,

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.600
and that is in case we have another 9/11.

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:46.930
But for training, it looks to me

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:50.798
like it's really Harriers
flying by this helicopter.

00:18:50.798 --> 00:18:52.156
Harry: Recommend using your Mavericks

00:18:52.156 --> 00:18:53.589
to take out the bridge.

00:18:53.589 --> 00:18:56.439
Pilot: Roger that. Lime
flight, switch Mavericks.

00:18:56.439 --> 00:18:57.272
Pilot: Two.

00:18:57.272 --> 00:19:00.163
Vincent: Two. OK.

00:19:00.163 --> 00:19:02.138
You know, normally you do wanna

00:19:02.138 --> 00:19:04.020
acknowledge a call that's made,

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:05.923
but they just felt very scripted.

00:19:05.923 --> 00:19:08.480
[jet engine roaring]
[missiles firing]

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:10.840
You see the Harriers peel off right away.

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:13.090
Presuming they're the ones
designating the target,

00:19:13.090 --> 00:19:14.260
they just need to make sure that

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:17.280
the laser is continuously on
the bridge the entire time.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:18.500
And then when it hits,

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:21.700
yeah, I mean, these could be
miniatures they're blowing up.

00:19:21.700 --> 00:19:23.220
It looks fairly realistic.

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:27.640
The Maverick AGM-65 was
designed to go after tanks,

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:29.200
so it's got pretty good punch,

00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:31.970
and it's going for armored
targets, so against cement,

00:19:31.970 --> 00:19:33.663
yeah, it might do pretty well.

00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:36.370
Of course, the Harrier keeps flying later.

00:19:36.370 --> 00:19:39.930
You've got Dana, the
daughter of the hero here,

00:19:39.930 --> 00:19:42.690
and some more gunfire and AIM-9 shots.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:43.860
Really love this scene.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:47.785
Overall, I'm gonna give it a
six out of 10 for "True Lies."

